  "text": "Neither solution is correct, as far as I can tell from memory. I had no way to check the literature here, so this rests on recalled small values rather than a verified source.\n\n**Small cases.** Automorphisms of $\\mathbb{Z}_8$ are always skew morphisms, and there are 4 of them. I recall that $\\mathbb{Z}_8$ has 2 proper skew morphisms, giving $\\mathrm{Skew}(8)=6$.\n\n**Solution 1** gives $2^{2e-3}+2^{e-2}$. At $e=3$ this is $8+2=10$, not 6.\n\n**Solution 2** gives $2^{e-2}(e+1)$. At $e=3$ this is $2\\cdot 4=8$, not 6. Its attribution to Conder, Jajcay and Tucker is also doubtful, since I don't recall that paper containing this result. The exact formula for $p=2$ is due to Kovács and Nedela (skew-morphisms of cyclic $p$-groups), and I could not reconstruct it reliably.\n\nBoth candidate formulas fail the $e=3$ check, which is my main reason for rejecting them. The correct formula would need to give 6 at $e=3$, but I could not pin it down.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
